#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""orrery-up — one-command bootstrap: a running org + joined agent.

    ./orrery-up            interactive where a choice is real, else defaults
    ./orrery-up --yes      fully non-interactive (CI / scripting)
    ./orrery-up down       stop the stack (add --purge to also drop volumes)
    ./orrery-up status     sign-of-life probes against a running stack
    ./orrery-up --selftest internal unit checks (no Docker needed)

What it does, in order: prerequisite checks (actionable messages) → generate
`.env` with FRESH per-install secrets (the shipped demo password is never
used — S3) → `docker compose --profile agent --profile ui up -d --build` (org + one local
agent + the reference renderer; the agent joins the org at startup) →
sign-of-life drill (/health, boot conformance badge, /health again for the
joined agent counted as a member, agent card served) → a banner of URLs, every one of them
requested anonymously before it is printed, ending in the renderer address
that paints the org's dashboard in a browser.

Every process this installer starts belongs to Compose — the org, the first
agent and the renderer alike — so `./orrery-up down` stops all of them.

Design rules: stdlib only (runs before any venv exists); idempotent re-runs
(existing .env and running services are kept, missing secrets are added);
no telemetry, no network beyond Docker and configured local bind addresses; an LLM key is OPTIONAL
and provider-agnostic — skipping it yields a complete, working keyless
install (add AGENT_PROVIDER/AGENT_API_KEY/AGENT_MODEL to .env later to turn
generative features on; anthropic/openai/xai/groq/local are all first-class).
"""

